WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.560
Take on the fastest guns in the West in Wild Gunmen.

00:00:19.080 --> 00:00:25.780
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:25.780 --> 00:00:27.040
I'm Mike Esposito.

00:00:27.280 --> 00:00:28.140
I'm Joe Castu.

00:00:28.260 --> 00:00:29.960
I'm Sean. And I'm Sam.

00:00:30.220 --> 00:00:33.280
I take my last name cue from you when you go past you.

00:00:33.280 --> 00:00:34.260
Oh, that's very nice. Yeah.

00:00:34.760 --> 00:00:35.820
Just so everyone knows.

00:00:36.040 --> 00:00:37.480
No, that was an important interruption.

00:00:37.880 --> 00:00:38.980
Right. Good. Well, good.

00:00:39.100 --> 00:00:45.900
All right. Okay. So today we're talking about Wild Gunmen, our last NES Zapper game for the launch games.

00:00:45.900 --> 00:00:51.120
There will be other NES Zapper games, but this one's kind of, I feel like, the most American

00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:52.080
of the three.

00:00:52.300 --> 00:00:56.580
Not only because it takes place in the Wild West, but it just feels like very much in the

00:00:56.580 --> 00:01:00.480
spirit, you know, with the saloon and just the, I don't know.

00:01:00.520 --> 00:01:05.940
You know, like it has, it has a lot of like cinema, like Western cinema aspects to it and

00:01:05.940 --> 00:01:07.520
stuff like that. Yeah. It's a spaghetti Western.

00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:08.640
I drew it. Yeah.

00:01:08.720 --> 00:01:10.540
Oh yeah. You're in the, you're in the West.

00:01:10.660 --> 00:01:13.280
You can see the, I forget what the rock is called, but the famous rock.

00:01:13.900 --> 00:01:15.120
The, oh yeah, you're right.

00:01:15.120 --> 00:01:16.220
You do see that in the background.

00:01:16.360 --> 00:01:21.840
Yeah. You even hear some of that like American music of like, you know, that's very American.

00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:23.960
That is right. Yeah.

00:01:24.080 --> 00:01:26.400
That is. That's like, it's like close to the national anthem.

00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:28.580
It's like right up there.

00:01:28.740 --> 00:01:30.500
I'm surprised there were no bald eagles in this game.

00:01:30.800 --> 00:01:31.720
There should have been, right?

00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:34.880
You might be tempted to shoot them and that'd be, that'd be bad.

00:01:34.880 --> 00:01:36.500
So that's, that's my first impression.

00:01:36.740 --> 00:01:39.820
I'm really shocked that like right off the bat that there were no bald eagles.

00:01:39.980 --> 00:01:43.960
No, no. My, my first impression was just that this game was seen to be targeted more towards Americans,

00:01:43.960 --> 00:01:48.820
but I know that the Japanese did like the wild west and Western movies in general, just

00:01:48.820 --> 00:01:50.040
like popular around the world.

00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:52.680
I mean, the spaghetti Westerns themselves are Italian made.

00:01:52.860 --> 00:01:56.360
Yeah. I believe that like this game actually did better in Japan though.

00:01:56.660 --> 00:01:59.620
Yeah. Because of it's like over the top American feel.

00:01:59.780 --> 00:02:04.760
Uh, that's why the original zapper looked like a wild, like a, like a Luger or whatever.

00:02:04.880 --> 00:02:06.060
Everybody wants to be a cowboy.

00:02:06.360 --> 00:02:08.120
Yeah. We all do.

00:02:08.820 --> 00:02:12.980
Well, I mean like when you're a kid, who doesn't want to just wrangle up some cows,

00:02:13.240 --> 00:02:15.860
you know, call it a day, maybe have a duel at night.

00:02:16.160 --> 00:02:17.700
Have a duel. I thought it was during a day.

00:02:18.160 --> 00:02:19.200
It's at high noon.

00:02:20.260 --> 00:02:21.920
Yeah. Which is, which is a good movie.

00:02:22.240 --> 00:02:24.460
Specifically at night. High noon's a good movie.

00:02:24.560 --> 00:02:25.660
I like that movie. Both versions.

00:02:30.640 --> 00:02:32.020
So what is Wild Gunman?

00:02:32.340 --> 00:02:34.300
Wild Gunman is a zapper game.

00:02:34.300 --> 00:02:35.960
Uh, essentially there's three different modes.

00:02:36.240 --> 00:02:40.520
One is a one on one duel where, uh, you can't draw before your opponent does.

00:02:40.860 --> 00:02:43.940
Seems a little cheap that I can't call out draw as well.

00:02:44.240 --> 00:02:46.360
Yeah. Exactly. I think I have to wait for him to draw.

00:02:46.760 --> 00:02:50.020
Uh, then there's another mode where there's two guys drawing against you and I've just

00:02:50.020 --> 00:02:52.360
never heard of that in the cowboy ethics.

00:02:52.820 --> 00:02:54.440
Never heard of it. You still have to wait.

00:02:54.660 --> 00:02:56.260
Yeah. You still have to wait for both of them.

00:02:56.800 --> 00:03:01.260
I assumed it was like a third party who was like overseeing the whole thing who was

00:03:01.260 --> 00:03:02.420
yelling out draw. Okay.

00:03:02.460 --> 00:03:04.060
Yeah. I think that you're onto something.

00:03:04.300 --> 00:03:09.500
Yeah. And then lastly, there's the, uh, you know, the more epic style of like, uh, you've learned

00:03:09.500 --> 00:03:14.840
the duels. Now it's time to do a shootout, uh, in a saloon with a whole gang of guys.

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:16.820
Uh, and who knows when they'll appear.

00:03:17.060 --> 00:03:21.420
This is more of like the stereotypical, uh, zapper kind of game that we would see even

00:03:21.420 --> 00:03:27.180
on like other consoles, other, uh, computer games that even have this, uh, you know, the

00:03:27.180 --> 00:03:31.000
cowboy setting, uh, was very popular for those kinds of zapper games.

00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:34.720
And there was one, uh, like mad dog that I played on windows 95.

00:03:34.920 --> 00:03:35.880
That was really cool.

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:40.880
Cause it had, um, real life animate, like, you know, it was filmed and then you would like

00:03:40.880 --> 00:03:41.740
react to that.

00:03:42.060 --> 00:03:43.300
So that was cool. Great act.

00:03:43.360 --> 00:03:48.700
But I always, the acting is, uh, well, mad dog himself, pretty scary, but I'm mad dog

00:03:48.700 --> 00:03:52.980
Mattis, general mad dog Mattis, uh, in the game, in person, in the flesh.

00:03:52.980 --> 00:03:57.040
Uh, but no, that's, that's wild gunman in a nutshell.

00:03:57.140 --> 00:04:01.120
And, uh, the game is, is as simple as I described it.

00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:02.740
So let's just kind of real fast.

00:04:02.940 --> 00:04:03.780
First impressions.

00:04:10.600 --> 00:04:14.440
My, my first impression was that it, that it was pretty simple, but also that it did

00:04:14.440 --> 00:04:17.240
a pretty good job of getting your adrenaline pumping a little bit.

00:04:17.380 --> 00:04:21.160
It, you know, it made you want to, it made you want to beat the, uh, the speed of the

00:04:21.160 --> 00:04:22.760
next guy, but you know, it's all about timing.

00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:25.220
So it kind of got your heart racing a little bit.

00:04:25.420 --> 00:04:30.640
Okay. Sean. Uh, my first impression of this game is that it's just a re skin of Hogan's alley.

00:04:31.100 --> 00:04:36.220
Ooh. Um, I mean, I don't know the exact timeline of, of when these games came out in Japan.

00:04:36.220 --> 00:04:41.720
Maybe Hogan's alley came out after this game in Japan, but these are the same game.

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:46.880
There's a timing mode, which is in, in Hogan's alley is just the gun ranch.

00:04:47.200 --> 00:04:51.260
And in this game, it's the actual standoffs and the shootouts.

00:04:51.260 --> 00:04:56.060
And then there's a shooting gallery mode where they just pop up at random moments.

00:04:56.200 --> 00:05:02.680
Like, I don't see much of a difference between them except for recontextualizing why you're shooting these targets.

00:05:03.300 --> 00:05:06.140
Yeah. And just to note, I did not know this.

00:05:06.200 --> 00:05:07.400
I looked it up while you were talking.

00:05:07.860 --> 00:05:12.540
Wild Gunman came first, but only by, let's see, it came out in February of 84.

00:05:13.660 --> 00:05:15.840
Hogan's Alley comes out in June of 84.

00:05:16.100 --> 00:05:20.400
So not really, like, probably development-wise, they were being developed at the same exact time.

00:05:20.680 --> 00:05:26.560
Sure. My first impression was like, oh, this is where the guys from Duck Hunt in Smash 4 come from.

00:05:26.840 --> 00:05:28.140
You know? Right.

00:05:29.280 --> 00:05:30.940
And that was pretty much it.

00:05:31.040 --> 00:05:32.740
It's just kind of like, yeah, very basic.

00:05:33.040 --> 00:05:37.740
Shoot where you want the guy to be, and then he goes away, and then you wait for the next one to come out.

00:05:37.820 --> 00:05:40.160
I don't know. It was pretty simple.

00:05:40.460 --> 00:05:44.700
And one thing I want to add as part of my first impressions is that this was in Back to the Future 2,

00:05:45.200 --> 00:05:48.180
which is kind of a big deal for an NES game.

00:05:48.180 --> 00:05:55.300
Now, granted, it came out for the first system first, and that was kind of how it was featured in the Back to the Future 2 diner scene.

00:05:55.600 --> 00:06:01.100
But I think that's just a big thing, you know, for Nintendo to be featured in such a mainstream way.

00:06:01.260 --> 00:06:03.820
Like, even their commercials were limited at the time.

00:06:03.860 --> 00:06:08.560
And this is a game, this whole launch is only really happening in New York and L.A.

00:06:08.560 --> 00:06:12.040
So for it to be featured in Back to the Future, kind of a big deal.

00:06:12.220 --> 00:06:13.400
That's a pretty good gig. Yeah, pretty good gig.

00:06:13.620 --> 00:06:16.920
I don't think Back to the Future 2 came out during the NES launch.

00:06:17.080 --> 00:06:17.880
It was much later.

00:06:18.040 --> 00:06:19.020
Yeah. But still.

00:06:19.260 --> 00:06:23.400
And they were thinking that people in 2015 would be playing this game in our case.

00:06:23.980 --> 00:06:27.540
But it is in the retro diner, to be fair, in Back to the Future 2.

00:06:27.700 --> 00:06:30.420
That's true. Yeah, the 80s-themed diner, which is retro in the future.

00:06:30.500 --> 00:06:32.120
And also, the kids in the future didn't like it.

00:06:32.240 --> 00:06:34.320
Maybe a little negative. Well, yeah.

00:06:34.460 --> 00:06:36.140
So I don't think they would put it on their essential game.

00:06:36.140 --> 00:06:37.260
No, we should talk about that.

00:06:37.260 --> 00:06:39.760
No, I really do want to go to that place now.

00:06:39.820 --> 00:06:42.340
Yeah, right? Like an 80s team, arcade, and style.

00:06:42.340 --> 00:06:43.440
And see Jaws 3D.

00:06:43.700 --> 00:06:47.660
Yeah, exactly. Anyway, let's move on to...

00:06:47.660 --> 00:06:49.780
We talked about what the objective of the game is.

00:06:49.860 --> 00:06:51.520
Let's just move right into game A.

00:06:51.520 --> 00:06:53.140
That's the one-out law mode.

00:07:00.080 --> 00:07:01.380
Any thoughts on this?

00:07:01.380 --> 00:07:07.100
Yes. So, actually, I want to talk about how I also saw that it was very similar to Hogan's

00:07:07.100 --> 00:07:13.320
Alley. But I did notice that the big difference was I preferred Hogan's Alley because it also

00:07:13.320 --> 00:07:15.800
gave you... You needed to make a choice.

00:07:16.100 --> 00:07:18.020
There were innocent people you had to avoid.

00:07:18.600 --> 00:07:20.000
So this was kind of...

00:07:20.000 --> 00:07:22.860
I mean, it makes sense now that you mentioned that this came out before...

00:07:22.860 --> 00:07:25.600
Or this came out before Hogan's Alley.

00:07:25.800 --> 00:07:30.540
That it felt like that mode in Hogan's Alley stripped of one of its very few elements.

00:07:30.540 --> 00:07:34.940
Yeah, one thing I really want to touch on fast here is that if there's anything this

00:07:34.940 --> 00:07:38.400
game got right, it's the setting, the atmosphere, the music.

00:07:38.820 --> 00:07:39.940
Like, it's really cool.

00:07:40.040 --> 00:07:41.500
And yeah, it's cheesy now.

00:07:41.660 --> 00:07:44.680
But I still think it's really cool that they were able to, like...

00:07:44.680 --> 00:07:47.580
You know, from everything from the voice synthesis of the fire...

00:07:47.580 --> 00:07:51.040
Yeah. Which is really cool and way more advanced than Kung Fu's Laugh.

00:07:51.240 --> 00:07:54.280
Mm-hmm. Which was another voice synthesis thing.

00:07:54.460 --> 00:08:00.360
To have that in there, and then also to have, like, that, as you were talking, the classic

00:08:00.360 --> 00:08:02.880
Western scene with the Monument Valley and stuff like that.

00:08:02.980 --> 00:08:05.220
Like, that's really cool that they included all that stuff.

00:08:06.300 --> 00:08:11.240
Where Game A, and pretty much most of the game, falls flat, is that you don't have to

00:08:11.240 --> 00:08:13.100
directly hit your opponent.

00:08:13.100 --> 00:08:16.100
No. You just have to get it in that vicinity.

00:08:16.420 --> 00:08:22.040
I found out that as long as you're aiming at the rectangle on the screen that is, like,

00:08:22.040 --> 00:08:28.180
the playable area where the enemy walks around, as long as you hit that, any part of it, at

00:08:28.180 --> 00:08:30.480
the right time, then you win.

00:08:30.680 --> 00:08:31.800
Right. You're not gonna miss.

00:08:31.800 --> 00:08:34.500
It's specifically about timing and not at all about aim.

00:08:34.500 --> 00:08:37.400
Yeah. At least in the one player.

00:08:37.760 --> 00:08:38.720
Yeah. In the one enemy.

00:08:38.980 --> 00:08:43.160
No, absolutely. It's the, you can hit them on the right side or the left side as far as you want, as long

00:08:43.160 --> 00:08:44.380
as it's registering on the screen.

00:08:44.380 --> 00:08:45.240
It's more reflex.

00:08:45.440 --> 00:08:51.100
And yeah, it's more reflex, which would be great if the reflexes were all that challenging.

00:08:51.380 --> 00:08:55.540
Unfortunately, this was something that I thought after maybe, like, 10 rounds or something,

00:08:55.680 --> 00:08:57.020
the difficulty would stack up.

00:08:57.260 --> 00:09:00.640
It doesn't, it never really goes back up to 1.2 seconds.

00:09:00.960 --> 00:09:05.760
Mm-hmm. Uh, and this is all something that, like, you know, the computer, uh, it tells you what

00:09:05.760 --> 00:09:08.060
their reaction time's gonna be before they ever even shoot.

00:09:08.200 --> 00:09:12.900
It says they're gonna be 1.2 seconds all the way down to the fastest possible, 0.4.

00:09:13.200 --> 00:09:20.560
Yeah. The 0.4 is rare, and even 0.4, we, me and Joe, we averaged around, like, 0.32 as, like,

00:09:20.620 --> 00:09:21.760
our average shot.

00:09:21.760 --> 00:09:24.540
It was pretty rare that we got above 0.4.

00:09:24.700 --> 00:09:31.240
Right. I think that you do have to, uh, self-impose a rule that you have to, like, draw it from

00:09:31.240 --> 00:09:33.880
your belt. And we did that from your suggestion.

00:09:34.160 --> 00:09:35.500
Yeah. We actually did do that.

00:09:35.640 --> 00:09:38.940
I've said, I want to try what Sean did, and it did make it a little harder.

00:09:39.320 --> 00:09:44.360
Yeah. But only in the grand scheme of things, added another 0.1, 0.15 seconds to it.

00:09:44.480 --> 00:09:46.800
That's a fast draw. Thank you so much.

00:09:46.800 --> 00:09:51.140
But, uh, to be fair, uh, we were playing this on the Wii U Virtual Console version.

00:09:51.140 --> 00:09:53.280
I don't own a NES cart of...

00:09:53.280 --> 00:09:56.640
Well, here's the thing, though. Was your hand off the controller, or was it still...

00:09:56.640 --> 00:09:57.820
It was on the controller.

00:09:58.040 --> 00:10:02.960
Yeah, well, there you go. I don't have a holster for my Wiimote, but I also don't have a holster for my Zapper,

00:10:03.200 --> 00:10:04.420
because I don't own a holster, period.

00:10:04.860 --> 00:10:06.660
Well, we, okay, I think we need to make holsters.

00:10:06.920 --> 00:10:07.960
Yeah. This would be a better game with holsters.

00:10:07.960 --> 00:10:08.920
Yeah, Wiimote holsters.

00:10:09.160 --> 00:10:14.280
But, uh, no, I did love that when you said that in, um, DuckCon as, like, a way to impose

00:10:14.280 --> 00:10:15.080
your own restrictions.

00:10:15.420 --> 00:10:16.880
Yeah. And so we definitely tried that.

00:10:17.060 --> 00:10:20.180
Uh, Joe even watched me fail as I tried to shoot it behind my legs.

00:10:20.580 --> 00:10:24.140
Like, I was, like, I was looking away, and I was shooting through the legs.

00:10:24.340 --> 00:10:26.400
Unfortunately, I just was never able to connect with the TV.

00:10:27.040 --> 00:10:28.480
Yeah. I think that's hard.

00:10:28.800 --> 00:10:34.640
I think that, uh, what this game does much better than Hogan's Alley is, like, you said

00:10:34.640 --> 00:10:38.620
the things with the setting and all of the aesthetic details, but, like, just the context

00:10:38.620 --> 00:10:40.460
of why you're doing this.

00:10:40.940 --> 00:10:45.260
Like, if you, in Hogan's Alley, it's like, okay, you're at a gun range at Hogan's Alley.

00:10:45.260 --> 00:10:46.340
Yeah, you're at the FBI training.

00:10:46.740 --> 00:10:51.760
Mm-hmm. Um, but, like, in this one, like, it's not just, like, oh, because this is a training,

00:10:52.060 --> 00:10:53.220
we have a timer on you.

00:10:53.440 --> 00:10:55.460
No, this is an actual shootout.

00:10:55.780 --> 00:10:57.360
Right. It feels more exciting.

00:10:57.360 --> 00:10:58.800
It does feel a lot more exciting.

00:10:59.100 --> 00:11:03.560
And I don't know if this had anything to do with, like, loading times or anything like

00:11:03.560 --> 00:11:08.060
that, or just part of the atmosphere, but it gets annoying pretty fast how long it takes

00:11:08.060 --> 00:11:10.500
these guys to move on to the screen.

00:11:11.280 --> 00:11:12.520
Oh, but it builds anticipation.

00:11:13.100 --> 00:11:16.220
Yeah, no, but I'm just saying, like, it's really cool for the first round.

00:11:16.220 --> 00:11:21.440
Yeah. It's really boring by, like, the 30th confirmed kill, which are, they're not kills.

00:11:21.860 --> 00:11:23.520
No. They're not kills.

00:11:23.600 --> 00:11:25.460
Their hat falls off or their pants fall down.

00:11:25.460 --> 00:11:28.100
Yeah, it's always comedic, but I like to think of them as kills.

00:11:28.140 --> 00:11:32.200
Although, when you get shot, it plays the dun-dun-da-dun-dun-da-dun-da-dun-da.

00:11:32.240 --> 00:11:33.940
Yeah, it doesn't seem like these guys are playing all that fair.

00:11:34.180 --> 00:11:35.860
Yeah. Which would lead to why they're wanted.

00:11:36.160 --> 00:11:42.120
Yeah. Although, that music, though, the music that happens when you get shot is really good.

00:11:42.340 --> 00:11:44.520
Yeah. There's, like, all these different counter melodies in it.

00:11:44.860 --> 00:11:47.560
Yeah, I think the music in this game is pretty choice.

00:11:48.140 --> 00:11:49.140
It's very choice.

00:11:49.360 --> 00:11:50.320
Very choice music.

00:11:51.160 --> 00:11:54.460
But the guys, too, there's only, like, five of them.

00:11:54.680 --> 00:11:57.660
Yeah. But they're all funny in their own little ways.

00:11:58.320 --> 00:12:03.700
But, again, I think that the inclusion of, like, this is going to sound bad, but, like,

00:12:03.700 --> 00:12:08.200
the inclusion of, like, a hostage or something, like, something that you don't want to shoot

00:12:08.200 --> 00:12:10.800
would have made this game a lot more interesting.

00:12:10.800 --> 00:12:14.320
Because if we move on to game B here, two outlaws.

00:12:17.400 --> 00:12:20.940
The only difference is now, again, still no precision.

00:12:21.040 --> 00:12:23.980
You just have to shoot the right side of the screen.

00:12:24.540 --> 00:12:27.480
And the entirety of the right side of the screen counts for the guy on the right.

00:12:27.600 --> 00:12:29.760
And the entirety of the left side of the screen for the guy on the left.

00:12:29.880 --> 00:12:31.380
So it's a little more interesting.

00:12:31.660 --> 00:12:36.060
But to make up for that, because now there's two guys on the screen, the reaction times

00:12:36.060 --> 00:12:36.960
are also boosted.

00:12:36.960 --> 00:12:41.600
So the other thing that sort of adds a bit of complexity to it is they don't draw at

00:12:41.600 --> 00:12:45.000
the same time. If they drew at the same time, they'd just be like, wham, bam.

00:12:46.160 --> 00:12:48.980
But you actually have to pay attention to both of them.

00:12:49.280 --> 00:12:53.640
And I guess that's one thing to make it a little bit more interesting.

00:12:53.940 --> 00:12:55.520
Yeah. And they're not so far off, though.

00:12:55.720 --> 00:12:57.160
Yeah. I mean, like, sometimes they can be.

00:12:57.160 --> 00:13:00.940
And really interestingly, I did like that sometimes one of them doesn't even shoot.

00:13:01.140 --> 00:13:02.040
Oh, at all. Yeah.

00:13:02.340 --> 00:13:06.400
And that was kind of cool, because it's like, if you're only listening for the fire, and

00:13:06.400 --> 00:13:11.820
you have a guy ready on one side, you might accidentally shoot innocent, I guess, even

00:13:11.820 --> 00:13:12.840
though they're unwanted posers.

00:13:13.000 --> 00:13:16.260
Yeah, they're unwanted posers, and they come out in the middle of a shootout with you and

00:13:16.260 --> 00:13:17.340
